binary triples governed by global URIs?

17
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:36,000
Cross these two axes, and you get four
legitimate industrial-grade architectures.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
None of them is wrong.

19
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:43,280
Yet in practice, choosing the wrong
quadrant for your organizational reality

20
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,640
has bankrupted digital thread initiatives.

21
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:52,780
To see why, we will not drown you in
hundreds of equipment classes. We will use

22
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:59,220
our compact domain vocabulary, vehicle,
functional slot, part, warehouse, and

23
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:03,960
defect, to run a single lifecycle stress
test across all four quadrants.

24
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:08,500
Let us set the stage on the canvas.

25
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:20,780
At time T equals 10, suspension arm ARM_SN_404 
is registered and placed inwarehouse_1.

26
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:29,760
At time T equals 20, it is removed from storage 
and mounted into the front suspension slot of vehicle_1.

27
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:36,920
At time T equals 50, a routine inspection
logs a minor defect, a 2 millimeter crack.

28
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:43,740
Finally, at time T equals 80, the crack
propagates to a critical 15

29
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:50,280
millimeters. The arm is dismantled from
vehicle_1 and returned to warehouse_1 for

30
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:51,680
a warranty investigation.

31
00:02:54,180 --> 00:03:01,000
Let us enable Show Closed History and step
into quadrant one, BFO in 3D combined

32
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,480
with a labeled property graph.

33
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:08,820
Look at the canvas in BFO plus LPG mode.

34
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,920
Why is this first quadrant the darling of
internal R&D software teams?

35
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:19,880
Because it aligns with everyday human
intuition.

36
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:27,380
Vehicle_1, the functional slot, ARM_SN_404,

37
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:34,440
and warehouse_1 sit on the canvas as
single permanent boxes, material entities.

38
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:39,680
Even though the suspension arm moved from
the warehouse to the vehicle and back,

39
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:44,880
there is still only one node for
ARM_SN_404.

40
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:48,240
Where does the history live?

41
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,480
Look directly at the edges.

42
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:56,520
Because an LPG engine natively supports
key-value properties on relationships, the

43
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,840
temporal intervals sit right on the
arrows.

44
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:05,320
Mounted_in carries valid from T20, valid
to T80.

45
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:11,060
Let us evaluate quadrant one against our
engineering criteria.

46
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:16,800
First, query ergonomics and computational
performance.

47
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:23,040
Look at the Cypher query in the lower
right panel. It is remarkably compact.

48
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:29,080
Because LPG databases use index-free
adjacency, traversing from vehicle_1

49
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:35,040
through the slot to the part and filtering
by edge timestamps takes microseconds.

50
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:40,180
Second, IT and developer onboarding.

51
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:46,640
Any software engineer familiar with JSON
and object-oriented programming can

52
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:48,580
understand this graph in an afternoon.

53
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,400
So where does quadrant one break down?

54
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,260
It hits a wall in two specific boundary
cases.

55
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:03,540
Boundary case one, multi-supplier data federation.

56
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:12,320
Suppose ARM_SN_404 is manufactured by a
tier-one supplier

57
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:18,760
in Poland, assembled by an OEM in Germany,
and serviced by a fleet operator in Sweden.

58
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:27,140
In an LPG database, node labels and edge properties 
are local strings, not globally dereferencable URIs.

59
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:32,880
When the supplier exports their internal
LPG data, their property is called

60
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:37,240
floor_length, while your Cypher query
expects crack.

61
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:43,860
There is no built-in W3C semantic layer to
align vocabularies or validate schemas

62
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:45,140
across company borders.

63
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:50,920
You end up writing brittle custom Python
ETL scripts for every single supplier.

64
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:56,920
Boundary case two, 
state-dependent property evolution.

65
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:01,780
In BFO 3D, the part node is timeless.

66
00:06:02,516 --> 00:06:08,796
When the crack grows from 2 millimeters at
T50 to 15 millimeters at T80, or when the

67
00:06:08,796 --> 00:06:12,776
surface temperature of the part
fluctuates, attaching those changing

68
00:06:12,776 --> 00:06:16,476
values to a single 3D continuant node
becomes awkward.

69
00:06:17,176 --> 00:06:21,076
You are forced to spawn separate quality
nodes for every measurement episode.

70
00:06:23,636 --> 00:06:27,416
To solve the cross-supplier
interoperability problem while keeping our

71
00:06:27,416 --> 00:06:32,216
3D intuitive worldview,
enterprises often move to quadrant two,

72
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:37,616
BFO in 3D combined with W3CRDF triples.

73
00:06:39,836 --> 00:06:43,956
Watch what happens to the exact same
dataset when we switch the physical

74
00:06:43,976 --> 00:06:46,036
perspective to physical RDF.

75
00:06:48,736 --> 00:06:54,996
The clean 3D graph suddenly explodes into
a web of yellow hexagons and white literal ovals.

76
00:06:55,756 --> 00:06:57,676
Why did the graph triple in size?

77
00:07:00,596 --> 00:07:05,376
This is the classic friction between a 3D
ontology and a pure triple store.

78
00:07:06,976 --> 00:07:11,694
In BFO, two three-dimensional objects, the
part and the slot, 

79
00:07:11,694 --> 00:07:16,756
are connected over a specific time window 
from T20 to T80.

80
00:07:18,516 --> 00:07:23,496
In LPG, we simply tucked T20 and T80
inside the edge.

81
00:07:24,976 --> 00:07:31,516
But in strict W3CRDF, edges are binary
predicates. A predicate cannot hold

82
00:07:31,516 --> 00:07:36,296
properties. You cannot attach a timestamp
to an arrow in standard RDF.

83
00:07:37,176 --> 00:07:44,136
Therefore, to express ARM_SN_404 was
mounted in the slot from

84
00:07:44,136 --> 00:07:50,696
T20 to T80, the database must reify the
relationship. It creates an artificial

85
00:07:50,696 --> 00:07:56,696
intermediate node, an RDF statement
hexagon, and fires out four separate triples:

86
00:07:57,336 --> 00:07:59,616
RDF subject pointing to the part,

87
00:08:00,296 --> 00:08:06,016
RDF object pointing to the slot, valid
from pointing to the integer literal 20,

88
00:08:06,536 --> 00:08:09,636
and valid to pointing to the integer
literal 80.

89
00:08:11,596 --> 00:08:15,096
Let us score quadrant two against our
operational criteria.

90
00:08:16,836 --> 00:08:17,836
Where does it shine?

91
00:08:19,436 --> 00:08:23,836
In semantic governance and NATO or
defense-grade standardization.

92
00:08:24,556 --> 00:08:31,196
Every class, every BFO relation, and every
unit of measure has a globally unique URI.

93
00:08:31,896 --> 00:08:36,536
You can hand a formal OWL ontology and
SHACL validation shapes to 20

94
00:08:36,536 --> 00:08:40,776
international contractors, and an
automated reasoner will mathematically

95
00:08:40,776 --> 00:08:44,976
verify whether their delivered data
complies with your contract before a

96
00:08:44,976 --> 00:08:46,936
single byte enters your repository.

97
00:08:49,796 --> 00:08:53,016
Where does quadrant two become an
operational nightmare?

98
00:08:54,816 --> 00:08:58,016
Look at the SPARQL query panel and the
relationship store.

99
00:09:01,056 --> 00:09:06,356
First, every time a technician logs an
assembly action, the database writes five

100
00:09:06,356 --> 00:09:08,816
to six triples instead of one edge.

101
00:09:11,596 --> 00:09:18,136
Second, to answer a simple temporal BOM
query at time T, the query engine cannot

102
00:09:18,136 --> 00:09:23,816
simply hop from node to node. It must
execute expensive relational joins across

103
00:09:23,816 --> 00:09:27,396
thousands of reified statement nodes and
literal values.

104
00:09:30,136 --> 00:09:35,656
Third, try explaining to a pragmatic
manufacturing IT supplier why they cannot

105
00:09:35,656 --> 00:09:41,056
just send you a parent-child table and why
they must instantiate blank reification

106
00:09:41,056 --> 00:09:43,196
nodes for every bolt in an assembly.

107
00:09:43,856 --> 00:09:47,596
The cognitive tension with traditional IT
vendors is immense.

108
00:09:50,496 --> 00:09:55,616
Now, what if our engineering problem is
not just tracking where a part is, but

109
00:09:55,616 --> 00:10:00,716
auditing the exact historical configuration 
of a complex system when a failure occurred?

110
00:10:02,916 --> 00:10:04,656
This brings us to quadrant three,

111
00:10:05,456 --> 00:10:11,276
ISO 15926 in 4D combined with a labeled
property graph.

112
00:10:13,016 --> 00:10:20,036
Let us switch the ontology to ISO 15926
and the perspective to physical LPG.

113
00:10:22,316 --> 00:10:27,416
Look closely at the topology on the
canvas. The philosophical shift is radical.

114
00:10:29,056 --> 00:10:34,535
In ISO 15926, the blue node, ARM_SN_404_4D, 

115
00:10:34,535 --> 00:10:42,256
is no longer a 3D physical 
piece of metal sitting on a shelf.

116
00:10:43,016 --> 00:10:44,956
It is the whole life individual,

117
00:10:45,616 --> 00:10:50,576
the entire four-dimensional spacetime worm
of that suspension arm from the day it

118
00:10:50,576 --> 00:10:54,236
was cast in the foundry to the day it is
melted down as scrap.

119
00:10:56,756 --> 00:11:03,216
And notice, the 4D worm itself never participates 
directly in any assembly or defect.

120
00:11:05,496 --> 00:11:11,596
Instead, every time reality changes, at
T10 in the warehouse, at T20 in the

121
00:11:11,596 --> 00:11:18,176
vehicle, at T80 back in the warehouse, the
system slices a new green or gray state

122
00:11:18,176 --> 00:11:22,256
node linked to the 4D parent via temporal
part of.

123
00:11:24,676 --> 00:11:26,616
Look at where the timestamps live now.

124
00:11:28,536 --> 00:11:34,976
The edges, mounted_in, has_slot,
stored_at,

125
00:11:35,476 --> 00:11:39,096
are completely clean. They carry no
timestamps at all.

126
00:11:39,716 --> 00:11:46,256
Instead, the time window, T20 to T80, is
an intrinsic property of the state node

127
00:11:46,256 --> 00:11:53,216
itself, and physical LPG edges called
transition_to link consecutive states

128
00:11:53,216 --> 00:11:54,736
into a chronological chain.

129
00:11:56,756 --> 00:12:00,116
Why would an engineering organization
choose quadrant three?

130
00:12:01,816 --> 00:12:06,576
Because it makes historical state auditing
and configuration management bulletproof.

131
00:12:08,844 --> 00:12:15,564
In a 3D graph, if vehicle_1 undergoes 50
component swaps, reconstructing the

132
00:12:15,564 --> 00:12:21,304
exact state of the vehicle at time T50
requires evaluating overlapping intervals

133
00:12:21,304 --> 00:12:22,624
on dozens of edges.

134
00:12:23,424 --> 00:12:27,744
In ISO 15926 plus LPG,

135
00:12:27,784 --> 00:12:33,369
a state is an immutable first-class node.
The 2 millimeter crack at T50 

136
00:12:33,369 --> 00:12:40,504
is permanently bound to the specific 
temporal slice of ARM_SN_404 that

137
00:12:40,504 --> 00:12:45,824
existed while it was mounted in the
temporal slice of vehicle_1. When the part

138
00:12:45,824 --> 00:12:53,264
moves to the warehouse at T80, a new state slice 
is born, linked by a direct transition_to pointer.

139
00:12:56,084 --> 00:13:01,884
For an internal digital twin running on a
high-performance LPG engine like Neo4j or

140
00:13:01,884 --> 00:13:08,344
Memgraph, traversing state transitions
along transition_to edges is blazingly

141
00:13:08,344 --> 00:13:12,284
fast, and historical data corruption is
structurally impossible.

142
00:13:15,444 --> 00:13:17,364
Where does quadrant three collapse?

143
00:13:18,864 --> 00:13:21,144
Look at the node store table under the
bonnet.

144
00:13:24,524 --> 00:13:27,704
This is the infamous 4D state explosion.

145
00:13:29,964 --> 00:13:35,904
In our tiny toy example with one vehicle,
one part, and three lifecycle events, we

146
00:13:35,904 --> 00:13:37,864
already have over a dozen nodes.

147
00:13:38,664 --> 00:13:45,024
Now imagine a real railway locomotive or
an aircraft with 50,000 serialized parts.

148
00:13:45,864 --> 00:13:51,004
Every time a maintenance technician swaps
a single sensor or updates a calibration parameter,

149
00:13:51,704 --> 00:13:57,484
strict 4D mereology dictates that a new
temporal state slice must be created, not

150
00:13:57,484 --> 00:14:02,444
only for the sensor, but for the sub-assembly, 
the module, and the vehicle itself.

151
00:14:03,424 --> 00:14:09,124
Within a year of fleet operation, your LPG
database is flooded with tens of millions

152
00:14:09,124 --> 00:14:10,644
of historical state nodes.

153
00:14:11,344 --> 00:14:14,744
Furthermore, because it is stored in a
schema-light LPG,

154
00:14:15,364 --> 00:14:19,703
if a junior developer forgets to close a
previous state timestamp when creating a

155
00:14:19,703 --> 00:14:25,564
new slice, the 4D worm forks into parallel
timelines, and there is no formal

156
00:14:25,564 --> 00:14:28,824
ontological reasoner in LPG to catch the
paradox.

157
00:14:30,824 --> 00:14:33,204
Finally, let us enter quadrant four,

158
00:14:34,104 --> 00:14:40,964
ISO 15926 in 4D combined with W3CRDF
triples.

159
00:14:42,764 --> 00:14:48,844
Switch the perspective to physical RDF
while staying in ISO 15926.

160
00:14:50,644 --> 00:14:51,644
Look at the canvas.

161
00:14:52,384 --> 00:14:57,024
This is the heavy artillery of industrial
knowledge architecture, born in the

162
00:14:57,024 --> 00:15:00,704
offshore oil, gas, and nuclear engineering
mega projects.

163
00:15:01,504 --> 00:15:07,684
Here, every 4D whole-life individual,
every temporal state slice, every defect

164
00:15:07,684 --> 00:15:12,134
classification, and every literal
timestamp is decomposed 

165
00:15:12,134 --> 00:15:15,124
into pure immutable W3C triples.

166
00:15:15,944 --> 00:15:24,684
Notice an intriguing detail when you compare 
BFO plus RDF against ISO 15926 plus RDF.

167
00:15:26,804 --> 00:15:33,644
In ISO 15926 plus RDF, we did not need
artificial RDF colon

168
00:15:33,724 --> 00:15:36,904
statement reification nodes for our
assembly relationships.

169
00:15:37,564 --> 00:15:43,764
Why? Because the 4D ontology already
turned time into a node, the state slice.

170
00:15:44,484 --> 00:15:51,084
Therefore, attaching ISO valid from and
ISO valid to as standard RDF literals

171
00:15:51,084 --> 00:15:55,364
directly to the state node is 100% native
to the triple model.

172
00:15:56,084 --> 00:16:01,044
The 4D ontology and the RDF triple store
are mathematically made for each other.

173
00:16:03,224 --> 00:16:06,144
When is quadrant four the only viable
choice?

174
00:16:07,884 --> 00:16:13,104
When you are building a multi-decade,
multi-consortium digital thread, such as a

175
00:16:13,104 --> 00:16:18,204
nuclear power plant, a national rail
infrastructure, or a defense fleet, where

176
00:16:18,424 --> 00:16:24,364
50 tier one contractors must exchange
configuration history over 40 years, long

177
00:16:24,364 --> 00:16:28,704
after today's software vendors and
proprietary databases have ceased to exist.

178
00:16:29,664 --> 00:16:32,464
Every state slice has a permanent URI.

179
00:16:33,424 --> 00:16:39,704
Reference data libraries, RDLs, define
every engineering concept globally. You

180
00:16:39,704 --> 00:16:44,844
can merge RDF graphs from 10 different
suppliers into a single repository without

181
00:16:44,844 --> 00:16:49,524
a single ID collision and run
mathematical reasoners over the entire

182
00:16:49,524 --> 00:16:51,784
40-year history of every part.

183
00:16:53,584 --> 00:16:55,684
And what is the price of quadrant four?

184
00:16:57,284 --> 00:17:00,924
Look at the sheer density of the diagram
and the SPARQL query.

185
00:17:03,584 --> 00:17:07,804
First, extreme cognitive and ontological
maintenance cost.

186
00:17:09,564 --> 00:17:12,944
You cannot hire regular web developers to
maintain this system.

187
00:17:13,604 --> 00:17:18,782
You need rare specialists who understand
both four-dimensional mereotopology 

188
00:17:18,782 --> 00:17:21,144
and W3C semantic web stack.

189
00:17:21,964 --> 00:17:26,444
Explaining to a mid-sized mechanical
supplier that they cannot simply report

190
00:17:26,444 --> 00:17:31,764
part A is in vehicle one, but must
instantiate a temporal part of triple

191
00:17:31,764 --> 00:17:37,184
linking two spatiotemporal extents to an
RDL class will stall your supply chain

192
00:17:37,184 --> 00:17:41,384
onboarding for months unless you build
thick translation middleware for them.

193
00:17:44,124 --> 00:17:46,884
Second, compute and query latency.

194
00:17:48,624 --> 00:17:54,224
Even a simple bill of materials tree
requires joining 4D individuals, active

195
00:17:54,224 --> 00:17:59,024
state slices, slot states, and literal
filters across millions of triples.

196
00:17:59,704 --> 00:18:04,624
Without aggressive caching or hybrid
views, real-time operational dashboards

197
00:18:04,624 --> 00:18:05,904
will grind to a halt.

198
00:18:08,764 --> 00:18:13,924
Let us return to the engineer viewpoint,
SMRM, to conclude.

199
00:18:15,144 --> 00:18:20,204
Notice that as soon as we click engineer
viewpoint, all four quadrants collapse

200
00:18:20,204 --> 00:18:24,444
back into the exact same clean three-node
engineering diagram.

201
00:18:25,324 --> 00:18:29,464
That is the ultimate lesson of IEC 63339.

202
00:18:30,984 --> 00:18:35,924
Your field technicians, CAD designers, and
fleet operators should never be forced to

203
00:18:35,924 --> 00:18:38,464
suffer the complexity of your storage
quadrant.

204
00:18:40,804 --> 00:18:45,004
If you are building a fast internal
engineering tracker inside one company,

205
00:18:45,644 --> 00:18:49,164
start in quadrant one with BFO and LPG.

206
00:18:50,044 --> 00:18:54,344
If you need strict cross-enterprise
vocabulary validation and semantic

207
00:18:54,344 --> 00:19:00,904
reasoning over intuitive 3D concepts,
choose quadrant two with BFO and RDF.

208
00:19:01,804 --> 00:19:06,644
If you need high-speed, uncorruptible
audit trails of complex state transitions

209
00:19:06,644 --> 00:19:13,144
inside a high-performance graph database,
build in quadrant three with 4D states in LPG.

210
00:19:13,944 --> 00:19:19,404
And if you are architecting a 30-year
multi-supplier industrial ecosystem where

211
00:19:19,404 --> 00:19:24,544
mathematical unambiguity across
organizations outweighs everything else,

212
00:19:24,544 --> 00:19:27,184
invest in the heavy machinery of quadrant
four,

213
00:19:27,844 --> 00:19:35,984
ISO 15926 over RDF, and hide that
complexity behind a clean SMRM user interface.
